topic("managerial", "Managerial & cost accounting", ["Financial Analysis", "Financial-Information Management"],
      "The internal numbers managers use: types of costs, break-even and marginal analysis, variance analysis, cost budgets and cost allocation.")

sub("cost-types", "Managerial accounting and types of costs", ["FI:660", "FI:658"],
    ["Managerial accounting produces information for people inside the business to plan, control and decide. Unlike financial accounting, which reports the past to outsiders under GAAP, managerial accounting looks forward, has no required format and can be as detailed as a decision needs: the cost of one product, the profit of one store, the payoff of one machine. It answers questions like 'Should we raise prices?', 'Which product line should we cut?' and 'Should we make this part or buy it?'",
     "Understanding cost types is the foundation. Costs behave differently as volume changes, relate to products differently and matter differently in decisions. The most common mistake in business decisions is letting sunk costs, money already spent that can't be recovered, drive a choice about the future. Only future costs that differ between options, differential costs, should matter."],
    ["Fixed costs stay the same as volume changes (rent, salaries, insurance); variable costs change with volume (materials, shipping, sales commissions); mixed costs have both parts (a phone plan with a base fee plus usage).",
     "Direct costs trace to a specific product (the flour in a cake); indirect costs, or overhead, are shared (the bakery's rent and utilities).",
     "Sunk cost: already spent and unrecoverable, so ignore it. Differential (incremental) cost: the difference between two options, so focus on it.",
     "Opportunity cost: the benefit given up by choosing one option over the next best.",
     "Product costs (materials, labor, manufacturing overhead) become inventory; period costs (marketing, administration) are expensed right away.",
     "Controllable costs can be influenced by a manager; uncontrollable ones can't, which matters when judging performance."],
    ["Example: a bakery's rent ($3,000 a month) is fixed and indirect; flour and eggs ($1.20 per cake) are variable and direct.",
     "Managerial reports can be daily or weekly; public companies' financial reports to outsiders are quarterly and annual (SEC)."],
    ["Companies often keep funding failing projects because of sunk costs, the 'Concorde fallacy', named for the supersonic jet that Britain and France kept funding despite clear losses.",
     "Amazon Web Services began as internal infrastructure; managerial analysis of its costs showed it could be sold to others at a profit."],
    ["Sunk costs are irrelevant to future decisions.", "Fixed cost per unit falls as volume rises, even though total fixed cost stays the same."],
    "\"The $15,000 already spent on the old software is a sunk cost. The real question is whether the new system's lower future costs beat the old one's.\"",
    [("Our client already spent $20,000 on a project that's struggling. Should they keep going?",
      "Ignore the $20,000. It's a sunk cost, and it's gone whatever they decide. The only question is whether the future benefits of continuing beat the future costs. If finishing needs $10,000 more and is likely to bring in $8,000, they should stop, even though it feels like wasting the first $20,000. Businesses that fall for the sunk-cost trap, like the Concorde program, lose more money. We'd decide on future numbers only."),
     ("How is managerial accounting different from financial accounting?",
      "Financial accounting reports the past to outsiders, like investors, lenders and the IRS, using GAAP rules and set formats. Managerial accounting helps insiders make future decisions, with no required format and as much detail as is useful, such as profit by product or store. For our client, the 10-K shows how the company did; a managerial report shows which product to push next quarter. Both matter, but managerial accounting drives daily decisions.")],
    [["IMA (management accountants)", "https://www.imanet.org"]])

sub("breakeven-marginal", "Break-even and marginal analysis", ["FI:659"],
    ["Break-even analysis finds the sales level where total revenue equals total costs, so profit is zero. Every unit sold contributes its contribution margin, price minus variable cost per unit, toward covering fixed costs; once fixed costs are covered, each extra unit's contribution is profit. Break-even units = fixed costs / contribution margin per unit. It's the first number any lender or investor asks for.",
     "Marginal analysis looks at the effect of one more unit, or one more decision, on revenue and cost. A business maximizes profit by producing until marginal revenue equals marginal cost. It's used for pricing, special orders (accept an order below normal price if it covers variable costs and doesn't use capacity needed elsewhere), make-or-buy decisions, adding a shift and deciding whether to drop a product. Because of diminishing returns, the cost of each extra unit eventually rises."],
    ["Contribution margin per unit = price - variable cost. Contribution margin ratio = CM / price.",
     "Break-even units = fixed costs / CM per unit. Break-even sales dollars = fixed costs / CM ratio.",
     "Target profit units = (fixed costs + target profit) / CM per unit.",
     "Margin of safety = actual (or expected) sales - break-even sales.",
     "Marginal rule: keep expanding while marginal revenue > marginal cost; stop when they're equal."],
    ["Food truck example: fixed costs $6,000 a month, average meal price $12, variable cost $5. CM = $7, so break-even is about 857 meals a month, roughly 29 a day over 30 days.",
     "To earn $3,000 a month in profit: ($6,000 + $3,000) / $7 = about 1,286 meals."],
    ["Airlines use marginal pricing: once a flight is scheduled, an empty seat's extra cost is small, so last-minute fares can be low as long as they cover that extra cost.",
     "Hotels and ride-share apps use dynamic pricing based on marginal demand, raising prices when demand outruns capacity."],
    ["Use the contribution margin, not the full price, in the break-even formula.", "Fixed costs don't change with a special order unless capacity changes."],
    "\"At $12 a meal with $5 of food cost, each meal contributes $7 to your $6,000 of monthly fixed costs. You break even at about 29 meals a day, and everything after that is profit.\"",
    [("How many units does our client need to sell to break even?",
      "Divide fixed costs by the contribution margin per unit. If monthly fixed costs are $6,000 and each $12 meal costs $5 to make, each sale contributes $7, so break-even is about 857 meals a month, around 29 a day. Then we'd check that's realistic: a busy lunch spot can serve 60. That margin of safety tells us the plan can work even in slow weeks. I'd sketch the break-even chart for you."),
     ("A big customer wants a large order at a 20% discount. Should we accept?",
      "Use marginal analysis. If the discounted price still covers the variable cost of each unit and we have idle capacity, the order adds profit, because fixed costs are already covered. A $10 product with a $6 variable cost sold at $8 still contributes $2 a unit. We'd decline if it needs overtime or crowds out full-price customers, or if other customers would demand the same discount.")],
    [SBA])

sub("variance", "Variance analysis and cost accounting budgets", ["FI:661", "FI:662"],
    ["A variance is the difference between budgeted (planned or standard) results and actual results. Favorable variances help profit (costs below plan, revenue above plan); unfavorable variances hurt it. Variance analysis asks why the difference happened and who can fix it, so managers can practice 'management by exception': spend time on the big surprises, not on everything that went to plan.",
     "Cost accounting budgets are the plans variances are measured against. They are built from standard costs, the expected price and quantity of materials and labor for each unit, and include the production budget, direct materials budget, direct labor budget and manufacturing overhead budget. A flexible budget adjusts these to the actual production level, so managers aren't blamed or praised just because volume changed."],
    ["Variance = actual - budget (or standard). Label each one favorable (F) or unfavorable (U).",
     "Materials: price variance = (actual price - standard price) x actual quantity; quantity variance = (actual quantity - standard quantity) x standard price.",
     "Labor: rate variance (paid more per hour) and efficiency variance (used more hours).",
     "Sales variances: price and volume.",
     "Investigate variances above a threshold, for example 5% or $1,000, and assign each to the manager who controls it.",
     "A favorable variance can still signal a problem, like cheaper materials that cause more defects."],
    ["Example: budgeted materials $10,000; actual $11,200, so the variance is $1,200 unfavorable. If the standard was 2,000 lbs at $5 and the business used 2,000 lbs at $5.60, it's all a price variance: renegotiate with the supplier.",
     "Companies in the S&P 500 explain differences between guidance (budget) and results on every quarterly earnings call."],
    ["When egg prices jumped during bird-flu outbreaks in 2022-2025, restaurants and bakeries saw big unfavorable materials price variances and responded with menu changes and surcharges.",
     "Toyota's standard-cost and continuous-improvement system uses daily variance tracking on the factory floor."],
    ["'Variance' means planned versus actual, the answer to many exam questions.", "Price and quantity variances can offset each other; always check both."],
    "\"Your food costs came in $1,200 over budget, all from egg prices. That's a price variance, so the fix is the supplier contract or the menu, not the kitchen staff.\"",
    [("How would you track whether the business is sticking to its budget?",
      "With a monthly variance report: budget, actual and the difference for each line, labeled favorable or unfavorable. We'd investigate anything over 5% and find the cause, such as price versus quantity for materials or rate versus hours for labor, then assign it to the manager who controls it. If materials ran $1,200 over because suppliers raised prices, the fix is renegotiating, not cutting staff. The report turns the budget into a monthly steering tool."),
     ("Is a favorable variance always good news?",
      "Not always. Spending less than budget on materials looks favorable, but if it came from cheaper, lower-quality inputs, defects and returns may rise later. A favorable marketing variance might mean campaigns were skipped and future sales will suffer. So we'd ask why every big variance happened, good or bad. The goal is better decisions, not just a prettier report.")],
    [["IMA (management accountants)", "https://www.imanet.org"]])

sub("allocation-abc", "Cost allocation, ABC and other uses of financial information", ["FI:663", "FM:016"],
    ["Cost allocation assigns shared, indirect costs, such as rent, utilities, IT and supervisors, to products, departments or customers using a cost driver, something that causes the cost, like machine hours, labor hours or square feet. Allocation matters because it changes how profitable each product looks, which affects pricing and which products to keep. Traditional costing uses one plant-wide rate; activity-based costing (ABC) uses many activities and drivers, such as setups, orders processed or inspections, for a more accurate picture.",
     "Financial information also supports non-traditional uses. Lean management uses cost data to remove waste (the Toyota Production System). Six Sigma uses data to cut defects to 3.4 per million opportunities, a method Motorola developed in 1986. Sustainability reporting measures environmental and social results such as carbon emissions, energy use and workforce data, alongside financial results. These approaches connect financial numbers to operations, quality and long-term value."],
    ["Steps: identify the cost pool, choose a driver, compute a rate (pool / total driver units), apply it to each product.",
     "ABC often shows that low-volume, complex products cost more than traditional costing suggests, and high-volume simple ones cost less.",
     "Lean's eight wastes: defects, overproduction, waiting, unused talent, transportation, inventory, motion, extra processing.",
     "Sustainability frameworks: GRI and the ISSB standards; in 2025 the EU moved to delay and narrow its CSRD reporting rules, and the SEC stopped defending its climate disclosure rule in March 2025.",
     "Six Sigma's DMAIC cycle: define, measure, analyze, improve, control."],
    ["Allocation example: $60,000 of overhead and 3,000 machine hours gives a rate of $20 per machine hour; a product that uses 2 hours carries $40 of overhead.",
     "Six Sigma's target is 3.4 defects per million opportunities (Motorola)."],
    ["General Electric under Jack Welch made Six Sigma company-wide in the 1990s and credited it with billions of dollars in savings.",
     "Many large companies, including Microsoft and Apple, publish annual sustainability or environmental reports with emissions data alongside their financial reports."],
    ["ABC uses multiple cost drivers; traditional costing uses one.", "Allocation doesn't change total costs, only how they're divided."],
    "\"When we allocate setup costs by activity, your custom orders turn out to cost $9 more each than you thought. That's why they've been quietly losing money.\"",
    [("Why might one of our client's products look profitable but actually lose money?",
      "Because of how overhead is allocated. If overhead is spread by sales volume, a small-batch custom product that needs lots of setups, design time and customer service gets too little of the cost. Activity-based costing assigns costs by what actually drives them, and often shows that complex, low-volume items are the money-losers. We'd run ABC on the top products, then raise prices or simplify the ones that don't cover their true cost."),
     ("How can financial information help a company beyond accounting?",
      "It can drive operations and strategy. Lean uses cost data to cut waste, like excess inventory that ties up cash. Six Sigma uses data to reduce defects, and GE credited it with billions in savings. Sustainability reporting tracks energy and emissions, which often cuts costs and meets what investors and big customers now ask for. For our client, measuring energy cost per unit could be the first step to both savings and a greener story.")],
    [["GRI standards", "https://www.globalreporting.org"], ["IFRS / ISSB", "https://www.ifrs.org"]])

