topic("law", "Law, compliance & ethics", ["Business Law", "Financial Analysis", "Risk Management"],
      "The laws and regulators of finance, compliance programs and technology, consumer protection and contracts, ethics in finance and accounting.")

sub("laws-regulators", "Major finance laws and regulators", ["BL:133", "FI:356"],
    ["Finance is heavily regulated because it handles other people's money and because failures spread through the whole economy. Most U.S. financial law was written after a crisis. The Great Depression brought the Securities Act of 1933 (honest disclosure for new securities), the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (created the SEC and regulates trading), the Glass-Steagall Act (separated commercial and investment banking, mostly repealed in 1999) and the FDIC. The 1940 Acts regulate mutual funds and investment advisers.",
     "Enron and WorldCom led to the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (2002). The 2008 crisis led to the Dodd-Frank Act (2010), which created the CFPB and the Financial Stability Oversight Council, added stress tests and the Volcker Rule limiting banks' own speculative trading. The GENIUS Act (2025) created the first federal framework for payment stablecoins. Regulators include the SEC and FINRA (securities), the Fed, OCC, FDIC and NCUA (banks and credit unions), the CFPB (consumer products), the CFTC (futures and derivatives), FinCEN (money laundering) and state insurance and banking departments."],
    ["Illegal securities practices: insider trading (trading on material non-public information), front-running (trading ahead of a client's order), churning (excessive trading for commissions), market manipulation (pump-and-dump), Ponzi schemes.",
     "Bank Secrecy Act (1970): banks file Currency Transaction Reports for cash over $10,000 and Suspicious Activity Reports.",
     "Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (1999): allowed banks, insurers and brokerages to combine, and requires privacy notices and data security.",
     "The CFPB's funding and size have been the subject of court fights since 2025; check its current status before citing it in a roleplay.",
     "Penalties: fines, disgorgement of profits, bans from the industry, prison."],
    ["Bernie Madoff's Ponzi scheme caused about $65 billion in paper losses; he was sentenced to 150 years in 2009 (U.S. Department of Justice).",
     "TD Bank pleaded guilty in 2024 to Bank Secrecy Act violations and agreed to pay about $3 billion, the largest such penalty against a U.S. bank (DOJ)."],
    ["Archegos Capital's 2021 collapse cost banks more than $10 billion; founder Bill Hwang was convicted of fraud and sentenced to 18 years in 2024.",
     "FTX founder Sam Bankman-Fried was sentenced to 25 years in 2024 for defrauding customers and investors (DOJ)."],
    ["The SEC regulates securities; FINRA is a self-regulatory organization overseeing brokers, under the SEC.", "Front-running uses a client's pending order; insider trading uses non-public company news."],
    "\"We're required to put your interests first, and you can check our record yourself on FINRA BrokerCheck in two minutes.\"",
    [("How do regulations protect our client?",
      "They make firms tell the truth and put clients first. Securities laws require honest disclosure, FDIC insurance protects deposits up to $250,000, Regulation Best Interest requires brokers to act in the client's best interest, and privacy laws protect their data. The client can check any advisor on FINRA BrokerCheck. When those rules are broken, the penalties are severe: TD Bank paid about $3 billion in 2024 for money-laundering failures. Regulation is the reason clients can trust the system."),
     ("Isn't all this regulation just extra cost for the business?",
      "It costs money, but it's far cheaper than the alternative. Compliance builds the trust that brings in customers, and violations cost much more: Wells Fargo paid $3 billion for its fake-accounts scandal and lost years of growth under a Fed asset cap. A firm with strong compliance can advertise it as a selling point. We'd treat compliance as part of the brand promise, not an afterthought.")],
    [SEC, FINRA, FINCEN])

sub("compliance", "Compliance programs and compliance technology", ["BL:148", "BL:149"],
    ["Compliance means making sure a firm and its employees follow laws, regulations and internal policies. In finance, a compliance department, led by a chief compliance officer, writes policies, trains employees, monitors activity, tests controls, investigates problems and reports to senior management and the board. Key areas include know-your-customer (KYC) identity checks, anti-money laundering (AML), suitability and best-interest rules, advertising rules, privacy, record-keeping and conflicts of interest.",
     "Compliance technology, or RegTech, automates much of this work. Software screens customers against sanctions lists, flags unusual transactions, archives every email and text, monitors trading for insider trading or manipulation, verifies identities with document scans and biometrics, and tracks new regulations. AI makes monitoring faster but adds its own duties: firms must be able to explain and test the models they use."],
    ["Elements of an effective program: written policies, a compliance officer, training, monitoring and auditing, reporting channels (a hotline), consistent discipline, and fixing problems that are found.",
     "KYC: verify identity, understand the customer's business and expected activity, and update over time.",
     "AML red flags: cash deposits just under $10,000 (structuring), rapid movement through many accounts, activity that doesn't fit the customer's profile.",
     "Record-keeping: brokers must keep business communications, including texts, which is why off-channel messaging became a major enforcement area.",
     "Compliance culture: leaders model it, employees can raise concerns without retaliation, and pay doesn't reward breaking rules."],
    ["The SEC and CFTC fined dozens of firms more than $2 billion combined from 2021 to 2024 for employees using unapproved texting apps like WhatsApp for business (SEC and CFTC).",
     "TD Bank's 2024 guilty plea found that most of its transactions went unmonitored for years, which let money launderers move hundreds of millions of dollars (DOJ)."],
    ["Banks use AI transaction monitoring to flag fraud in real time; card networks such as Visa report preventing billions of dollars in fraud each year with AI.",
     "Robinhood and other brokers have been fined by FINRA for weak supervision and customer-communication failures."],
    ["Compliance is ongoing monitoring, not a one-time checklist.", "Structuring (splitting cash deposits to avoid the $10,000 report) is itself a crime."],
    "\"Compliance isn't red tape; it's what lets clients trust us. Every account goes through identity checks, and our systems flag unusual activity within minutes.\"",
    [("How would you build a compliance culture at this firm?",
      "Start at the top and make it easy to do the right thing. Leaders talk about compliance and follow it themselves, and every employee gets short, practical training each year. There's an anonymous hotline with no retaliation, and pay never rewards breaking rules, which is what went wrong at Wells Fargo. Then technology monitors communications and transactions automatically. Culture plus monitoring catches problems early, when they're cheap to fix."),
     ("How can technology help a financial firm stay compliant?",
      "It automates the work humans can't do at scale. Software screens every customer against sanctions lists, flags suspicious transactions like repeated cash deposits just under $10,000, archives all emails and texts and monitors trading for insider activity. That matters because regulators fined firms over $2 billion for unrecorded texting. We'd pair it with human review, because a flagged alert still needs judgment.")],
    [FINRA, FINCEN, SEC])

sub("consumer-law", "Consumer financial law and contracts", ["FI:063", "BL:002"],
    ["Consumer financial products, such as loans, cards, bank accounts and investments, come with legal responsibilities on both sides. Providers must disclose costs clearly, treat customers fairly, protect their data and avoid unfair, deceptive or abusive acts and practices (UDAAP). Consumers are responsible for reading what they sign, paying as agreed, keeping records and reporting errors or fraud promptly, which protects their legal rights.",
     "Every financial product is a contract. A legally binding contract needs an offer, acceptance, consideration (something of value exchanged), parties with capacity (generally adults of sound mind), a legal purpose and genuine agreement. Minors can usually cancel (void) contracts, which is one reason lenders require adult cosigners for under-18s. Breaking a contract gives the other side remedies such as damages or, for loans, repossession or collection."],
    ["Truth in Lending Act / Regulation Z: APR and finance charge disclosure, card rules, right to cancel some home loans within 3 days.",
     "Truth in Savings Act / Regulation DD: banks must disclose APY and fees on deposit accounts.",
     "Electronic Fund Transfer Act / Regulation E: debit card and transfer protections and error resolution.",
     "Fair Credit Reporting Act, Equal Credit Opportunity Act, Fair Debt Collection Practices Act, CARD Act, Gramm-Leach-Bliley privacy notices.",
     "Consumers can file complaints with the CFPB, the FTC or their state attorney general; firms must respond."],
    ["The CFPB receives millions of consumer complaints a year, most about credit reporting (CFPB complaint database).",
     "Federal law caps credit card fraud liability at $50, and Regulation E caps debit losses at $50 if reported within 2 business days."],
    ["Overdraft fees became a major enforcement and policy issue; many large banks, including Capital One and Citi, eliminated overdraft fees on consumer accounts in 2022-2024.",
     "Courts have struck down contracts with minors or terms that were hidden or unconscionable, which is why clear disclosures matter."],
    ["Consideration means each side gives something of value.", "Truth in Lending is about credit; Truth in Savings is about deposit accounts."],
    "\"Before you sign, let's read the three numbers that matter: the APR, the total finance charge and any penalty fees. The law requires the lender to show you all three.\"",
    [("What responsibilities does our client have when using a financial product?",
      "Read the disclosures, pay on time, keep records and report problems fast. Lenders must show the APR and fees under the Truth in Lending Act, but the client has to actually read them. Reporting an unauthorized debit within two business days caps the loss at $50, and waiting can cost far more. Keeping statements and receipts means any dispute can be proven. Knowing their rights and duties protects them."),
     ("What makes a contract legally binding?",
      "Six things: an offer, acceptance, consideration (something of value exchanged), parties with legal capacity, a legal purpose and genuine agreement without fraud or pressure. A car loan has all six: the lender offers money, you accept, you exchange payments for the car, you're an adult, buying a car is legal and the terms are disclosed. If a 16-year-old signs alone, they lack capacity, so lenders require an adult cosigner.")],
    [CFPB, FTC])

sub("ethics", "Ethics in finance, accounting and financial information", ["FI:355", "FI:351", "FM:003", "RM:041"],
    ["Ethics is doing what's right when the law doesn't force you to, or when no one is watching. In finance, ethical duties include putting clients' interests first, disclosing conflicts of interest and fees, keeping information confidential, giving honest and fair advice, and never misleading anyone with numbers. A fiduciary must act in the client's best interest; brokers under Regulation Best Interest must act in the client's best interest when recommending, and disclose conflicts.",
     "In accounting and financial-information management, ethics means integrity, objectivity and independence: reporting numbers accurately even when they're bad news, not 'managing' earnings to hit targets, protecting data, and resisting pressure from bosses. Ethical failures in finance are especially damaging because trust is the product. When it's lost, firms collapse, as Enron's auditor Arthur Andersen did in 2002."],
    ["Quick ethics test: Is it legal? Is it fair to everyone affected? Would I be comfortable seeing it in the news? What would a trusted mentor say?",
     "Common conflicts: commissions that reward selling certain products, gifts from vendors, trading ahead of clients, recommending in-house funds.",
     "Professional codes: CFP Board's fiduciary standard, the CFA Institute Code and Standards, the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct.",
     "Whistleblowers who report securities fraud can receive 10%-30% of sanctions over $1 million through the SEC program, with legal protection from retaliation.",
     "Data ethics: use customer data only for its stated purpose, get consent and protect it."],
    ["The SEC whistleblower program has paid more than $2 billion in awards since 2011 (SEC).",
     "Form CRS, required since 2020, makes brokers and advisers give clients a short plain-English summary of services, fees and conflicts (SEC)."],
    ["Enron's collapse in 2001 erased about $74 billion of shareholder value and destroyed Arthur Andersen, one of the 'Big Five' accounting firms.",
     "Wells Fargo employees opened millions of fake accounts to meet sales goals, a case study in how incentives can push good people to act unethically."],
    ["Legal and ethical aren't the same; something can be legal and still unethical.", "Disclosing a conflict doesn't remove it; you still must act in the client's interest."],
    "\"We get paid a flat fee, not commissions, so we earn the same whichever fund we recommend. Our only incentive is your result.\"",
    [("How would you handle a conflict of interest with this client?",
      "Disclose it clearly, then put the client first anyway. If our firm pays a higher commission on its own funds, we'd tell the client that up front, show a lower-cost alternative side by side and let the numbers decide. That's what Regulation Best Interest and the CFP fiduciary standard require. Better yet, a flat-fee structure removes the conflict entirely. Trust is the product we're selling."),
     ("Your manager asks you to make this quarter's numbers look better. What do you do?",
      "Decline and explain why. Moving expenses or booking revenue early to hit a target is earnings manipulation, and it's how WorldCom and Enron started. I'd offer honest options instead, like explaining the miss with a clear recovery plan. If the pressure continued, I'd go to compliance or the audit committee, and the SEC whistleblower program protects people who report fraud. Short-term pressure is never worth the firm's integrity or my career.")],
    [SEC, CFP, ["CFA Institute ethics", "https://www.cfainstitute.org/standards/professionals/code-ethics-standards"]])

sub("accounting-law", "Legal considerations and technology in accounting", ["FI:353", "FI:352"],
    ["Accounting is governed by law as well as standards. Public companies must file financial statements with the SEC that follow GAAP and are audited by an independent CPA firm registered with the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board (PCAOB), created by Sarbanes-Oxley. Under SOX, the CEO and CFO personally certify the statements (Section 302), management must assess internal controls over financial reporting (Section 404), and knowingly certifying false statements can mean prison. Tax law, payroll law and record-retention rules apply to every business.",
     "Technology has transformed accounting. Cloud software like QuickBooks Online and Xero links to bank accounts and categorizes transactions automatically; large companies run enterprise resource planning (ERP) systems like SAP and Oracle. Automation handles invoices and reconciliations, data analytics let auditors test every transaction instead of a sample, AI drafts reports and flags anomalies, and SEC filings are tagged in XBRL so computers can read them. The trade-off is cybersecurity and data-quality risk."],
    ["SOX 302: CEO/CFO certify reports. SOX 404: internal control assessment. SOX 802: penalties for destroying records. SOX 906: criminal penalties for false certification.",
     "The SEC's clawback rule (effective 2023) requires listed companies to recover executive bonuses based on financial results that are later restated.",
     "Records: the IRS generally requires keeping tax records for at least 3 years; employment tax records for 4 years.",
     "Technology controls: user access limits, audit trails, backups, segregation of duties inside the software.",
     "Blockchain can create shared, tamper-resistant ledgers, and auditors increasingly test crypto holdings."],
    ["Sarbanes-Oxley was passed in 2002 after the Enron and WorldCom scandals (SEC).",
     "SEC filings have used XBRL data tagging since 2009, making financial data machine-readable (SEC)."],
    ["Wirecard, a German payments company, collapsed in 2020 after admitting that about 1.9 billion euros of cash on its balance sheet probably didn't exist; its auditor missed it for years.",
     "Big accounting firms now use AI to analyze entire general ledgers for unusual entries during audits."],
    ["The PCAOB oversees auditors of public companies; FASB sets accounting standards.", "SOX applies mainly to public companies, but its practices are often adopted by private firms."],
    "\"With cloud accounting connected to your bank, your books update daily, so you'll see a cash problem in days instead of finding it at tax time.\"",
    [("What legal risks should a growing company watch in its accounting?",
      "Accuracy, tax compliance and records. If it plans to go public, Sarbanes-Oxley will require the CEO and CFO to certify the statements and the company to test its internal controls, so building those controls now is cheaper. Every business must file accurate tax and payroll returns and keep records at least three years. Misstated numbers can bring penalties, restated bonuses under the SEC clawback rule and lost trust. Clean books are a legal shield."),
     ("How would you use technology to improve this business's accounting?",
      "Move to cloud accounting linked to the bank and payment apps, so transactions record automatically and the owner sees real-time reports. Automate invoicing and reminders to speed up cash collection. Add user permissions so the person entering bills can't approve them. That's segregation of duties built into the software. The time saved on data entry goes into analysis, like weekly cash forecasts.")],
    [SEC, PCAOB, ["FASB", "https://www.fasb.org"]])

