topic("industry", "Governance & the finance industry", ["Professional Development", "Financial Analysis", "Financial-Information Management"],
      "Corporate governance, convergence and consolidation in the industry, and fintech and AI.")

sub("governance", "Corporate governance", ["PD:213", "PD:214", "PD:301", "PD:302", "PD:303"],
    ["Corporate governance is the system of rules, practices and people that direct and control a company, balancing the interests of shareholders, managers, employees, customers, lenders and the community. Shareholders elect a board of directors, the board hires and oversees the CEO and sets strategy and risk appetite, and managers run daily operations. Good governance keeps managers accountable, decisions transparent and the company's long-term health ahead of short-term pressure.",
     "A well-governed company has an independent board with relevant expertise, key committees (audit, compensation, nominating and governance, often risk), a clear code of conduct, transparent and timely reporting, strong internal and external audit, and fair treatment of shareholders. Governance structures depend on ownership (public, private, family or founder-controlled), size, industry regulation, laws and stock exchange rules, culture and pressure from investors. Every employee has a role: following the code, keeping accurate records and speaking up about problems."],
    ["NYSE and Nasdaq require a majority of independent directors and fully independent audit committees.",
     "Separating the CEO and board chair roles, or naming a lead independent director, adds oversight.",
     "Dual-class shares (Meta, Alphabet) give founders voting control with a minority of the equity, a governance trade-off.",
     "Governance shapes decisions: big moves need board approval, which slows decisions but improves them, and risk committees review major bets.",
     "Employee's role: follow policies, report concerns through hotlines, avoid conflicts of interest, and keep records honest."],
    ["About 85% of S&P 500 directors are independent (Spencer Stuart U.S. Board Index).",
     "Sarbanes-Oxley requires audit committees of listed companies to be made up entirely of independent directors (SEC)."],
    ["In November 2023 OpenAI's nonprofit board fired CEO Sam Altman, then reinstated him within days after employees and investors revolted, a case study in how governance structure shapes control.",
     "WeWork's 2019 IPO collapsed after filings revealed weak governance, including founder control and conflicts of interest; its valuation fell from $47 billion to under $10 billion."],
    ["The board oversees; management manages.", "Independent directors have no material ties to the company or its managers."],
    "\"Good governance means someone independent is checking the decisions. For your family business, an advisory board with two outside members would add that discipline.\"",
    [("Why does corporate governance matter to investors and clients?",
      "Because it decides whether the people running the company are held accountable. An independent board, honest reporting and a strong audit committee make fraud and reckless bets less likely. Investors pay more for well-governed companies. WeWork's valuation fell from $47 billion to under $10 billion when weak governance came out before its IPO. For our client, good governance means their investment is protected by more than the CEO's word."),
     ("What can an ordinary employee do to support good governance?",
      "Follow the code of conduct, keep records accurate and speak up. Most fraud is caught by tips, so an employee who reports a problem through the hotline is part of governance. Employees should also disclose conflicts of interest, like a vendor who's a relative, and never cut corners to hit a target. When everyone does that, the board's oversight actually works.")],
    [SEC, ["NACD (directors)", "https://www.nacdonline.org"]])

sub("consolidation", "Convergence and consolidation in the finance industry", ["FI:573"],
    ["Convergence is the blurring of lines between types of financial firms: banks sell insurance and investments, insurers offer savings products, brokerages offer checking accounts and tech companies offer payments and loans. The Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act of 1999 made this legal by repealing parts of Glass-Steagall, right after the 1998 Citicorp-Travelers merger created Citigroup, a 'financial supermarket.' Today, fintechs and big tech firms are converging into finance too.",
     "Consolidation is the shrinking number of firms through mergers and acquisitions. The U.S. had more than 17,000 FDIC-insured institutions in 1984 and 4,238 in mid-2026. Drivers include economies of scale (spreading technology and compliance costs over more customers), geographic reach, diversification, deposits for funding and rescues of failing banks. Benefits include efficiency and broader services; costs include less competition, fewer local banks, job losses and 'too big to fail' risk."],
    ["Horizontal merger: two firms in the same business (two banks). Conglomerate or cross-industry: a bank buying an insurer.",
     "Benefits to customers: one-stop shopping, bigger ATM networks, better technology. Risks: higher fees, less personal service, branch closures.",
     "Regulators (Fed, OCC, FDIC, Department of Justice) review bank mergers for competition and safety.",
     "Systemically important banks face stricter capital rules and annual stress tests.",
     "Community banks compete through relationships, local decision-making and niche lending."],
    ["FDIC-insured institutions fell to 4,238 in the second quarter of 2026; 36 merged with other banks in that quarter alone (FDIC).",
     "Capital One completed its roughly $35 billion acquisition of Discover in May 2025, combining a card issuer with a payment network."],
    ["JPMorgan Chase bought First Republic Bank in May 2023 after it failed, making the largest U.S. bank even larger.",
     "In 2023 UBS took over Credit Suisse in a Swiss government-brokered rescue, merging two of Europe's biggest banks."],
    ["Convergence = firms offering each other's products; consolidation = fewer, bigger firms.", "Gramm-Leach-Bliley (1999) enabled convergence; it didn't create the FDIC."],
    "\"Because of consolidation, the number of U.S. banks has fallen by about three-quarters since 1984. A local credit union can still give you the personal service big banks are losing.\"",
    [("How does consolidation in banking affect our client?",
      "There are pros and cons. Bigger banks offer better apps, more ATMs and more products under one roof, like Capital One adding the Discover network in 2025. But fewer competitors can mean higher fees, branch closures and less personal service, especially for small businesses that need a banker who knows them. We'd compare the big bank's convenience with a community bank or credit union's service, and pick based on what the client needs most."),
     ("Why do banks merge?",
      "Mainly for scale. Technology, cybersecurity and compliance costs are huge, and spreading them over more customers lowers the cost per account. Mergers also add deposits, new regions and new products, and sometimes rescue a failing bank, as when JPMorgan bought First Republic in 2023. The number of insured institutions fell from more than 17,000 in 1984 to 4,238 by mid-2026, and 36 merged in one recent quarter alone.")],
    [["FDIC BankFind", "https://banks.data.fdic.gov"], ["Federal Reserve History", "https://www.federalreservehistory.org"]])

sub("fintech-ai", "Fintech, AI and technology in financial services", ["FM:011", "RM:042", "FI:352"],
    ["Fintech is technology that delivers financial services in new ways: mobile banking, peer-to-peer payments (Venmo, Zelle), robo-advisors (Betterment, Wealthfront, Schwab Intelligent Portfolios), buy now, pay later, neobanks (Chime), online lenders, crypto exchanges and stablecoins. It has lowered costs, made accounts cheaper to open and brought services to people traditional banks underserved. Most fintechs partner with chartered banks, so regulation and FDIC coverage still flow through the bank.",
     "Artificial intelligence is now built into finance: fraud detection that scores each card swipe in milliseconds, chatbots and virtual assistants, credit underwriting with more data, trading algorithms, compliance monitoring and drafting reports. The risks are real: biased models that discriminate, privacy breaches, errors or 'hallucinations', and models no one can explain. Fair-lending law still requires lenders to give specific reasons when they deny credit, even if an AI made the decision."],
    ["Robo-advisors build and rebalance diversified portfolios automatically for fees often around 0.25% a year or less.",
     "Banks' AI fraud tools analyze location, device, amount and habits to flag unusual activity in real time.",
     "Model risk management: test AI models for accuracy and bias, document them, monitor them and keep humans in the loop.",
     "The GENIUS Act (July 2025) set federal rules for payment stablecoins, including full reserves in cash or short-term Treasuries.",
     "Cybersecurity is the flip side: more digital services mean more attack surface."],
    ["Bank of America's virtual assistant Erica has handled billions of client interactions since its 2018 launch (Bank of America).",
     "The SEC approved the first spot bitcoin ETFs in January 2024, bringing crypto into regulated brokerage accounts (SEC)."],
    ["The 2024 Synapse collapse showed fintech's middleman risk: when a banking-as-a-service provider failed, customers of several apps lost access to their funds for months.",
     "JPMorgan Chase says it spends more than $15 billion a year on technology, part of why big banks are investing heavily in AI."],
    ["A fintech app usually isn't a bank; FDIC coverage depends on its partner bank.", "AI decisions in lending still must follow fair-lending laws."],
    "\"A robo-advisor can automate your investing for about 0.25% a year, while we focus on the human parts: your goals, your fears and the big decisions.\"",
    [("Why should our client pay for a human advisor when robo-advisors are so cheap?",
      "For simple investing, a robo-advisor at about 0.25% a year is excellent, and we'd say so. A human adds value where judgment and emotion matter: coordinating taxes, insurance, estate planning and big life decisions, and keeping the client from panic-selling in a crash. Studies by firms like Vanguard estimate that behavioral coaching alone can add meaningful value. Many clients use both: a robo-advisor for the portfolio and an advisor for the plan."),
     ("What are the risks of banks using AI?",
      "Bias, errors, privacy and accountability. An AI trained on biased data could deny loans unfairly, which is illegal under fair-lending laws, and lenders must still explain every denial. Models can make confident mistakes, and they rely on sensitive personal data. Good practice is testing models for bias, keeping humans in the loop for big decisions and strong cybersecurity. Used that way, AI catches fraud faster while customers stay protected.")],
    [["CFPB: AI and credit decisions", "https://www.consumerfinance.gov"], ["Bank for International Settlements fintech", "https://www.bis.org"]])

