topic("client", "Client service & careers", ["Customer Relations", "Professional Development"],
      "A finance professional's responsibilities to clients, CRM technology, handling difficult situations, and careers and networking in finance.")

sub("client-duty", "Responsibilities of finance professionals to clients", ["CR:012"],
    ["Finance professionals hold clients' money and futures in their hands, so the job comes with clear responsibilities. First, know the client: their goals, timeline, income, assets, debts, risk tolerance and family situation. Second, act in their best interest and recommend only what fits. Third, communicate clearly: explain options, risks and costs in plain language, without jargon. Fourth, protect their information and keep it confidential. Fifth, follow through, with timely service, documented decisions and regular reviews.",
     "Legal standards back these duties up. Registered investment advisers and CFP professionals are fiduciaries. Brokers must follow Regulation Best Interest. Every firm must give clients Form CRS, a short summary of services, fees and conflicts. Professionals must also watch for signs of elder financial abuse and may add a 'trusted contact' to accounts, which FINRA Rule 4512 encourages. Great client service goes beyond the rules: it's being the person the client trusts when things go wrong."],
    ["Discovery meeting: ask open questions ('What would financial security look like for you?'), listen more than talk, and summarize back.",
     "Suitability and best interest: match products to the client's profile, and document why.",
     "Disclose fees and conflicts up front, in dollars, not just percentages.",
     "Confidentiality: Gramm-Leach-Bliley privacy rules; never discuss a client's affairs without permission.",
     "Ongoing duty: annual reviews, updates after life events, prompt answers, and education so clients understand their plan.",
     "The Senior Safe Act (2018) protects trained employees who report suspected elder financial exploitation in good faith."],
    ["Form CRS has been required for brokers and investment advisers since June 30, 2020 (SEC).",
     "People 60 and older reported about $3 billion in fraud losses to the FTC in 2025 (FTC), which is why trusted-contact forms matter."],
    ["The 2026 FTDM district case asked advisors to explain the responsibilities of finance professionals to nervous new parents, a sign of how central this PI is.",
     "Many firms now hold 'family meetings' that include adult children, with the client's permission, to plan for aging and reduce exploitation risk."],
    ["A fiduciary standard is stricter than a suitability standard.", "Confidentiality has limits: suspected fraud and legal orders can require disclosure."],
    "\"Our responsibility is simple: understand your goals, recommend only what's in your best interest, explain every cost in dollars and check in with you at least once a year.\"",
    [("What are your responsibilities to us as our financial advisor?",
      "Four promises. We'll get to know your goals and situation before recommending anything. We'll act in your best interest, as fiduciaries, and disclose every fee and conflict in plain dollars. We'll keep your information confidential and secure. And we'll stay with you: an annual review and a call after any big life event, like a new baby or job change. You can verify our record on FINRA BrokerCheck anytime."),
     ("How would you earn the trust of a client who has been burned before?",
      "Listen first, then make everything transparent. We'd ask what went wrong last time and promise not to repeat it. Show every fee in dollars, explain recommendations in plain language and put the plan in writing. Start with small, low-risk steps, like building an emergency fund, so they see results before bigger decisions. Then follow through on every promise, on time. Trust is rebuilt by consistency, not by a pitch.")],
    [FINRA, ["SEC Form CRS", "https://www.investor.gov/CRS"], CFP])

sub("crm", "Customer relationship management (CRM) and technology", ["CR:024", "CR:016", "CR:017", "CR:018", "NF:124"],
    ["Customer relationship management is the strategy of building long-term, profitable relationships with clients by understanding and serving them better than competitors. CRM technology, software like Salesforce Financial Services Cloud, Wealthbox or Redtail for advisors, stores every client's profile, accounts, goals, documents, meeting notes and interactions in one place, so anyone on the team can give informed, personal service.",
     "CRM systems also automate work: reminders for annual reviews and birthdays, tasks after a meeting, segmenting clients by needs, tracking referrals and measuring satisfaction. Analytics can flag clients who might leave or who would benefit from a new service. Ethics matter: client data must be collected with consent, used only for its purpose, kept secure and never used to push products clients don't need."],
    ["Track: contact details, family members, goals, risk profile, accounts, meeting notes, preferences (text vs call), life events.",
     "Automate: review reminders, follow-up emails, document requests, compliance logs of client communications.",
     "Segment: by life stage (new parents, pre-retirees), by service level, by product need.",
     "Measure: retention rate, referrals, Net Promoter Score, response time.",
     "Ethics and law: privacy notices, data security, consent for marketing, no misleading personalization.",
     "Advanced database use: a CRM is a relational database; queries, filters and reports pull lists such as 'clients over 60 with no trusted contact' or 'families whose child turns 18 this year', and dashboards join account data with interaction history."],
    ["Bain & Company research found that raising customer retention by 5% can raise profits by 25% to 95%, a classic case for CRM.",
     "Salesforce is among the most widely used CRM platforms, and its Financial Services Cloud is built for banks, advisors and insurers."],
    ["USAA is known for top customer-satisfaction scores, partly because its systems give every representative a full view of the member's accounts and history.",
     "Advisors who log life events in CRM can reach out proactively, for example reviewing insurance when a client's child turns 16 and starts driving."],
    ["CRM is a strategy supported by technology, not just software.", "Retention is usually cheaper than winning new customers."],
    "\"We'd log your goals and key dates in our CRM, so we'll reach out before your daughter starts college, not after the bills arrive.\"",
    [("How would you use technology to keep this client relationship strong?",
      "With a CRM that remembers everything and prompts action. We'd record their goals, family, preferences and every conversation, then set automatic reminders for annual reviews and key life events, like a child's 16th birthday for car insurance or college deadlines. The system would flag if they haven't heard from us in 90 days. That turns a once-a-year relationship into proactive service, which is what keeps clients and earns referrals."),
     ("What ethical issues come with collecting so much client data?",
      "Privacy, security and purpose. We'd collect only what's needed to serve the client, explain how it's used in our privacy notice, protect it with encryption and access limits, and never sell it or use it to push products they don't need. Under Gramm-Leach-Bliley, financial firms must give privacy notices and safeguard data. A breach or misuse would destroy the trust the CRM is supposed to build.")],
    [["Salesforce Financial Services Cloud overview", "https://www.salesforce.com/financial-services/"], CFPB])

sub("difficult-clients", "Handling difficult clients, complaints and cultural differences", ["CR:009", "CR:010", "CR:019"],
    ["Difficult moments in finance usually come from fear or frustration: a market drop, a denied loan, a fee they didn't expect, a mistake by the firm. The goal is to calm the situation, solve the real problem and keep the relationship. A simple model is LAST: Listen fully without interrupting, Apologize for the experience (even if it isn't your fault), Solve the problem or explain the next steps, and Thank them for raising it. Staying calm, using their name and not taking anger personally are essential.",
     "Clients also differ by culture, language, age, financial experience and beliefs about money. Some cultures avoid debt or interest; some families make money decisions together; some clients prefer formal communication. Adapting means asking about preferences instead of assuming, avoiding jargon and idioms, using translated materials or interpreters, respecting family roles and checking understanding. That's good service and, under fair-lending and fair-treatment rules, a legal duty too."],
    ["Complaint steps: listen, acknowledge, apologize, investigate, solve or escalate, follow up, document.",
     "Never argue, blame the client or make promises you can't keep; offer choices.",
     "Escalate when needed: a supervisor, compliance (if it involves a possible rule violation) or the formal complaint process.",
     "Market-panic calls: acknowledge the fear, remind them of the plan and their timeline, show history, and agree on a next step.",
     "Cultural adaptation: plain language, interpreter services, materials in the client's language, flexible meeting formats, awareness of religious finance needs (e.g. interest-free Islamic finance products)."],
    ["Companies must respond to complaints filed through the CFPB portal, generally within 15 days (CFPB).",
     "About 1 in 5 U.S. residents speaks a language other than English at home (U.S. Census Bureau)."],
    ["During the March 2020 crash, advisors who called clients first, before clients called them, kept far more clients from selling at the bottom.",
     "Some banks and credit unions now offer Sharia-compliant home financing for Muslim customers who avoid paying interest."],
    ["Apologizing for the experience isn't admitting legal fault.", "Adapting to culture means asking, not stereotyping."],
    "\"I completely understand why seeing that drop is scary. Let's look at your plan together: this money isn't needed for 20 years, and every past drop like this has recovered.\"",
    [("The market just fell 15% and our client wants to sell everything. What do you say?",
      "First acknowledge the fear: 'It's completely normal to feel this way.' Then remind them of their plan: this money is for retirement in 25 years, and their emergency fund covers short-term needs. Show history: the S&P 500 fell about 37% in 2008 and 18% in 2022 and recovered both times, while people who sold locked in losses. Offer a concrete step, like rebalancing or keeping their monthly investments going at lower prices. Calm, specific and empathetic keeps them on track."),
     ("A client is angry about a fee they didn't expect. How do you handle it?",
      "Listen fully, apologize for the surprise and investigate right away. If the fee was our mistake or poorly explained, reverse it and fix the disclosure. If it was correct, explain clearly what it's for and show ways to avoid it, like setting up a minimum balance or autopay. Then follow up in writing and log it in the CRM so it doesn't happen again. A complaint handled well often makes a client more loyal than one who never had a problem."),
     ("How would you adapt your advice for a client from a different cultural background?",
      "Ask about their preferences instead of assuming. Some clients want family members in the meeting, some prefer materials in another language, and some avoid interest for religious reasons, so we'd offer options like interest-free financing where available. We'd use plain language, check understanding often and respect how they make decisions. Good advice only works if the client trusts and understands it.")],
    [CFPB, ["Consumer complaint database", "https://www.consumerfinance.gov/data-research/consumer-complaints/"]])

sub("careers", "Careers and professional relationships in finance", ["PD:152", "PD:153"],
    ["Finance offers careers in five big areas. Banking: tellers, personal bankers, loan officers, branch managers, commercial lenders. Investments: financial advisors and planners, research analysts, portfolio managers, traders, investment bankers. Insurance: agents, underwriters, claims adjusters, actuaries. Accounting: staff accountants, auditors, tax professionals, forensic accountants. Corporate finance: financial analysts, treasury, FP&A, controllers and CFOs. Growing fields include fintech, compliance, risk management, data analytics and cybersecurity.",
     "Professional relationships drive finance careers because the industry runs on trust and referrals. Ways to build them: internships, DECA and other student organizations, informational interviews, LinkedIn, professional associations (the Financial Planning Association, CFA societies, state CPA societies), mentors and alumni networks. Licenses and credentials signal skill and ethics: the SIE exam is open to anyone 18 or older, then Series 7 and 66 for brokers and advisors, state insurance licenses, and the CPA, CFP and CFA designations."],
    ["Entry paths: business, finance, accounting or economics degrees; many banks hire high-school graduates as tellers with tuition help.",
     "Key skills: analysis, communication, ethics, Excel and data tools, relationship building, staying calm under pressure.",
     "Credentials: CFP (planning, fiduciary), CFA (investment analysis), CPA (accounting, licensed by states), FRM (risk), insurance licenses.",
     "Networking: give before you ask, follow up within 24 hours, keep in touch, and thank people who help.",
     "Professional image: online presence, handshake and eye contact, punctuality, clear writing."],
    ["Personal financial advisors and financial analysts earned median pay of about $100,000 in 2024, and accountants about $80,000 (BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook).",
     "The SIE (Securities Industry Essentials) exam can be taken at 18 without sponsorship from a firm (FINRA)."],
    ["Many top banks, including JPMorgan Chase and Goldman Sachs, recruit summer interns heavily from the year before graduation and make many full-time offers from those programs.",
     "Bank of America and others run programs that hire high-school graduates and pay for further education."],
    ["The CPA is a state license; the CFP and CFA are professional certifications.", "Networking is building relationships over time, not just asking for jobs."],
    "\"As DECA members, we've learned that finance runs on relationships: we'd connect you with a CPA for taxes and an estate attorney, and coordinate everyone on one plan.\"",
    [("What career in finance interests you, and why?",
      "Financial planning, because it combines analysis with helping real people. Preparing for this event showed us how a plan for a family, like an emergency fund, the right insurance and automatic investing, changes their future. Planners earn a median of about $100,000, and the CFP fiduciary standard fits how we want to work. We'd start by passing the SIE, then intern with a planning firm. It's a career where trust is the product."),
     ("How would you build professional relationships in the finance industry?",
      "Start early and give value first. DECA, internships and informational interviews with alumni let us meet professionals now. We'd follow up within a day, thank people and stay in touch with useful updates, not just requests. Joining groups like the Financial Planning Association's student chapters builds a network that leads to referrals and mentors. In finance, your network is often how clients and opportunities find you.")],
    [BLSOOH, CFP, ["FINRA SIE exam", "https://www.finra.org/registration-exams-ce/qualification-exams/securities-industry-essentials-exam"]])

