topic("fraud", "Fraud & identity protection", ["Financial Analysis", "Risk Management"],
      "Protecting against identity theft, recognizing and stopping scams, and preventing fraud inside a business.")

sub("identity", "Protecting against identity theft and securing online accounts", ["FI:073", "FI:830"],
    ["Identity theft is when someone uses your personal information, such as a Social Security number, account number, login or medical ID, to open accounts, make purchases, file tax returns or get medical care in your name. It can take months to discover and years to clean up. Children are targets too, because their clean credit files often go unchecked until they apply for a first loan. Synthetic identity fraud mixes real and fake details to create a new 'person.'",
     "Prevention is a layered habit. Freeze credit at all three bureaus (free, and you can unfreeze in minutes when applying for credit). Use a password manager with unique passwords and app-based multifactor authentication. Turn on account alerts, review statements weekly, shred documents, limit what you share on social media and avoid banking on public Wi-Fi. Get an IRS Identity Protection PIN to block fraudulent tax filings. If it happens, act fast with a recovery plan from IdentityTheft.gov."],
    ["Credit freeze: free at Equifax, Experian and TransUnion; blocks new accounts; doesn't affect your score.",
     "Fraud alert: lasts one year (seven for confirmed victims); lenders must verify your identity.",
     "MFA: an authenticator app or passkey beats a texted code, which can be stolen by SIM-swap attacks.",
     "IRS IP PIN: a six-digit number, available to anyone who verifies their identity, that's required to file a return in your name.",
     "If victimized: report at IdentityTheft.gov, freeze credit, change passwords, contact affected companies, file a police report if needed, and check credit reports."],
    ["The FTC received more than a million identity theft reports in 2024 (FTC Consumer Sentinel Network).",
     "The 2017 Equifax breach exposed personal data on about 147 million Americans (FTC)."],
    ["In 2024 a breach at National Public Data exposed records including Social Security numbers for a huge number of people, another reason experts recommend freezing credit by default.",
     "SIM-swap criminals have stolen millions in crypto by taking over victims' phone numbers and intercepting text codes."],
    ["A freeze stops new accounts; it doesn't stop misuse of existing cards.", "Checking your own credit never hurts your score."],
    "\"Three steps today: freeze your credit at all three bureaus, turn on app-based two-factor login for your bank and email, and set alerts for every transaction.\"",
    [("What's the single best thing our client can do to protect their identity?",
      "Freeze their credit at Equifax, Experian and TransUnion. It's free, takes about 15 minutes online and stops criminals from opening new accounts in their name, and they can lift it in minutes when they apply for credit. After that, a password manager and app-based two-factor login on the bank and email accounts. The Equifax breach exposed 147 million people, so we assume data is already out there and lock the doors."),
     ("Our client's identity was just stolen. What should they do first?",
      "Act today. Go to IdentityTheft.gov for an FTC recovery plan and report, freeze credit at all three bureaus, and call the fraud departments of any affected accounts to close or flag them. Change passwords, starting with email, since it resets everything else. Then pull all three free credit reports and dispute anything fraudulent. An IRS IP PIN prevents tax-refund fraud next season. Moving fast limits the damage and their liability.")],
    [IDT, ["AnnualCreditReport.com", "https://www.annualcreditreport.com"], ["IRS Identity Protection PIN", "https://www.irs.gov/identity-theft-fraud-scams/get-an-identity-protection-pin"]])

sub("scams", "Recognizing and stopping scams", ["FI:073"],
    ["Scams trick people into handing over money or information themselves, which makes them harder to reverse than theft. The biggest categories today are investment scams (often crypto 'opportunities' that start on social media or dating apps, sometimes called 'pig butchering'), impostor scams (fake bank fraud departments, government agencies, tech support or a relative in trouble), romance scams, fake job offers, phishing texts and emails, and fake-check scams. AI now lets criminals clone voices and create convincing fake videos.",
     "Nearly every scam shares the same red flags: urgency ('act now'), secrecy ('don't tell your bank'), an unusual payment method (gift cards, crypto, wire transfers, payment apps, cash couriers), an unsolicited contact and a promise that's too good to be true. The best defense is a pause: hang up, look up the official number yourself and call back. Banks never ask you to move money to 'protect' it or to read them a one-time code."],
    ["Never pay with gift cards, crypto or wire transfers to someone who contacted you first.",
     "Verify independently: call the number on the back of your card or the official website, never the number in the message.",
     "Family code word: a phrase that proves a caller claiming to be a relative is real (defends against AI voice clones).",
     "Report scams to ReportFraud.ftc.gov, and internet crimes to the FBI's IC3.gov; call your bank immediately to try to stop payments.",
     "Older clients: add a trusted contact to accounts, and talk openly with family about common scams."],
    ["Americans reported a record $15.9 billion in fraud losses to the FTC in 2025, up about 25% from 2024; investment scams were about $7.9 billion, nearly half (FTC).",
     "Impostor scams were the most-reported type, with about $3.5 billion lost in 2025 (FTC).",
     "The FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center counted about $20.9 billion in reported losses for 2025 (FBI IC3)."],
    ["Pig-butchering scammers build relationships for weeks over text or dating apps, then steer victims to fake crypto trading sites that show phony gains until the victim tries to withdraw.",
     "In a widely reported 2024 case, an employee at the engineering firm Arup in Hong Kong sent about $25 million after a video call with deepfakes of company executives."],
    ["Your bank will never ask for a one-time passcode.", "Payment-app transfers you authorize are hard to reverse, even if you were tricked."],
    "\"If anyone pressures you to pay right now with gift cards, crypto or a wire, it's a scam. Hang up and call us or your bank at the number you already know.\"",
    [("How would you protect an older client from scams?",
      "With a mix of education and safeguards. We'd add a trusted contact to their accounts, which FINRA rules encourage, so we can call someone if something looks wrong. We'd set alerts and transfer limits, and agree on a family code word to defeat AI voice-clone calls. And we'd teach the three red flags: urgency, secrecy and unusual payment methods like gift cards or crypto. People 60 and older reported about $3 billion in losses in 2025, so this is real protection."),
     ("A client says someone from their bank called asking them to move money to a 'safe account.' What do you tell them?",
      "That it's a scam, and to stop right now. Real banks never ask you to move money to protect it or to share a one-time code. Hang up and call the number on the back of the card. If any money has been sent, call the bank's fraud line immediately to try to stop it, then report it at ReportFraud.ftc.gov. Impostor scams cost Americans about $3.5 billion in 2025, and the pause before acting is the best defense.")],
    [FTC, FBI, ["ReportFraud.ftc.gov", "https://reportfraud.ftc.gov"]])

sub("occ-fraud", "Fraud inside businesses (occupational fraud)", ["RM:058", "FI:351"],
    ["Occupational fraud is when employees, managers or executives misuse their position to steal from their own organization. The Association of Certified Fraud Examiners groups it into three types: asset misappropriation (stealing cash, inventory or using company cards for personal spending), the most common; corruption (bribes, kickbacks, conflicts of interest); and financial statement fraud (falsifying results), the least common but the costliest.",
     "The fraud triangle explains why it happens: pressure (debt, addiction, unrealistic targets), opportunity (weak controls) and rationalization ('I'll pay it back', 'they underpay me'). Businesses can't remove pressure, but they can remove opportunity with controls and reduce rationalization with a strong ethical culture. Most frauds are caught by tips from employees, customers or vendors, so a hotline is one of the most effective controls."],
    ["Controls: segregation of duties, dual approval, bank reconciliations by someone independent, surprise audits, mandatory vacations, job rotation, inventory counts.",
     "Tip hotline: anonymous, available to employees, vendors and customers.",
     "Red flags: living beyond means, refusing vacations, unusually close vendor relationships, control-override attempts.",
     "Data analytics: test 100% of transactions for duplicates, round numbers, weekend entries and Benford's Law anomalies.",
     "Fidelity bonds or crime insurance transfer some of the loss."],
    ["Organizations lose an estimated 5% of revenue to fraud each year; the median loss per case was $145,000 (ACFE 2024 Report to the Nations).",
     "Tips detected 43% of frauds, more than any other method, and the typical fraud lasted 12 months before discovery (ACFE 2024).",
     "Financial statement fraud was the costliest type, with a median loss of $766,000 (ACFE 2024)."],
    ["Small businesses with few employees are hit hard because one bookkeeper often handles everything; ACFE data shows small organizations suffer large median losses relative to their size.",
     "Rita Crundwell, comptroller of Dixon, Illinois, stole about $53 million over two decades by controlling a secret bank account; she was caught when a coworker covering for her vacation noticed it."],
    ["Asset misappropriation is the most common; financial statement fraud is the most expensive.", "Tips, not audits, catch the most fraud."],
    "\"Most fraud is caught by tips, so we'd set up an anonymous hotline and make sure the person who writes the checks never reconciles the bank account.\"",
    [("How would you protect this small business from employee theft?",
      "Remove the opportunity. Split duties so the person who pays bills doesn't reconcile the bank account; the owner can review the reconciliation monthly. Require two approvals for payments over a set amount, run surprise cash counts and make everyone take vacation, which is how the $53 million Dixon, Illinois, theft was finally caught. Add an anonymous tip line, since tips catch 43% of frauds. These controls cost little next to the median $145,000 loss."),
     ("Why do trusted employees commit fraud?",
      "The fraud triangle: pressure, opportunity and rationalization. A loyal employee facing big medical bills, with sole control of the books, might tell themselves they'll 'borrow' and repay. The business can't remove the pressure, but it can remove the opportunity with controls and reduce rationalization with a culture where ethics are discussed and help, like an employee assistance program, is available. Good people stay good when the system supports them.")],
    [ACFE, COSO])

# =====================================================================
topic("business", "Starting & running a business", ["Business Law", "Entrepreneurship", "Economics"],
      "Types of business ownership, entrepreneurship and the owner's role, and what drives profit and business risk.")

sub("ownership", "Types of business ownership", ["BL:003"],
    ["The form of ownership decides who controls the business, who is liable for its debts, how it's taxed and how it can raise money. A sole proprietorship is one owner, easy and cheap to start, but the owner has unlimited personal liability. A partnership has two or more owners sharing profits, control and, in a general partnership, unlimited liability; limited partnerships and LLPs limit some partners' liability. A partnership agreement is essential.",
     "A corporation is a separate legal entity owned by shareholders, with limited liability and the ability to sell stock, but more paperwork. A C corporation pays corporate tax and shareholders pay tax again on dividends; an S corporation passes income to owners to avoid double taxation but is limited to 100 shareholders and one class of stock. A limited liability company (LLC) combines limited liability with flexible pass-through taxation and is the most popular choice for small businesses today. Other forms include cooperatives, nonprofits and franchises."],
    ["Compare on five factors: liability, taxes, control, ability to raise money, continuity (does it survive the owner?).",
     "Sole proprietorship: simplest; profits on the owner's return (Schedule C); unlimited liability.",
     "General partnership: shared management and unlimited, joint liability; ends if a partner leaves unless the agreement says otherwise.",
     "C corp: unlimited growth through stock, double taxation, survives owners. S corp: pass-through, ownership limits.",
     "LLC: limited liability, pass-through by default, flexible management; can elect S corp taxation.",
     "Franchise: buy rights to a proven brand and system (fees and royalties, less independence)."],
    ["Sole proprietorships make up the majority of U.S. business tax returns, though corporations earn most business revenue (IRS Statistics of Income).",
     "An S corporation can have no more than 100 shareholders and only one class of stock (IRS)."],
    ["Apple began in 1976 as a partnership of Steve Jobs, Steve Wozniak and Ronald Wayne; Wayne sold his 10% stake for $800 within two weeks.",
     "Ben & Jerry's started as a partnership in a converted Vermont gas station in 1978 and later became a corporation to raise money for growth."],
    ["An LLC is not a corporation, though both offer limited liability.", "Limited liability protects personal assets but not against personal guarantees or fraud."],
    "\"Since you and your friend are starting together, we'd form an LLC with a written operating agreement. It protects your personal savings and spells out what happens if one of you leaves.\"",
    [("What type of business ownership would you recommend for our client, and why?",
      "An LLC, for most small businesses starting out. It protects the owners' personal assets from business debts and lawsuits, profits pass through to their personal returns so there's no double taxation, and the paperwork is light. If profits grow, the LLC can elect S corporation taxation to save on self-employment tax. With a partner, a written operating agreement covering ownership splits, decisions and buyouts is essential. That gives protection now and flexibility later."),
     ("Our client wants to go into business with a friend. What should they think about?",
      "Put everything in writing first. A partnership or LLC operating agreement should cover how much each person invests, how profits are split, who makes which decisions, what happens if someone wants out, and how disputes get resolved. In a general partnership, each partner can be personally liable for the other's business debts, which is why we'd recommend an LLC. Friendships survive businesses when expectations are clear from day one.")],
    [SBA, IRS])

sub("entrepreneur", "Entrepreneurship and the role of the owner", ["EN:039", "EN:040", "EN:044"],
    ["Entrepreneurship is recognizing an opportunity and organizing resources to pursue it, accepting risk in exchange for potential reward. Entrepreneurs range from 'lifestyle' small-business owners (a bakery, a landscaping company) to scalable startups aiming for rapid growth, social entrepreneurs who build businesses to solve social problems, and intrapreneurs who innovate inside existing companies. Small businesses create a large share of new jobs and drive competition and innovation in a market economy.",
     "The owner wears many hats, especially early on: visionary, salesperson, financial manager, hiring manager, compliance officer and risk manager. Key responsibilities include writing a business plan, securing funding, managing cash, meeting legal and tax obligations, hiring and leading people, and building customer relationships. Ethics in entrepreneurship, like honest marketing, fair treatment of employees and paying suppliers on time, builds the reputation small businesses depend on."],
    ["Common entrepreneur traits: initiative, resilience, comfort with risk, creativity, persistence, willingness to learn.",
     "A business plan covers: executive summary, product or service, market analysis, competition, marketing, operations, management, financial projections and funding needs.",
     "Validate before investing heavily: test with a small launch, pre-orders or a pop-up.",
     "Free help: SBA, Small Business Development Centers (SBDCs) and SCORE mentors.",
     "Ethics: honest claims, fair pay, data privacy, paying taxes and suppliers on time."],
    ["Americans have filed more than 5 million new business applications each year since 2021, well above pre-pandemic levels (U.S. Census Bureau Business Formation Statistics).",
     "Small businesses account for about 99.9% of U.S. businesses and employ nearly half of private-sector workers (SBA Office of Advocacy)."],
    ["Airbnb's founders started by renting air mattresses in their apartment during a 2007 design conference, a small test that validated a huge idea.",
     "TOMS Shoes popularized a 'one for one' social-entrepreneurship model, then later changed it as the company learned what helped communities most."],
    ["Entrepreneurs accept risk; that's what distinguishes them from managers.", "A business plan is for the owner's thinking as much as for lenders."],
    "\"Before you spend $50,000 on a storefront, let's test the idea with a $2,000 pop-up for three weekends. If customers buy, we'll have real numbers for the bank.\"",
    [("What's the biggest risk for this new business, and how do you reduce it?",
      "Running out of cash before the business proves itself. We'd reduce it three ways: test the idea cheaply first, with a pop-up or pre-orders, so the launch is based on real demand; keep startup costs lean by leasing equipment instead of buying it; and hold at least three to six months of expenses in reserve or an approved line of credit. We'd track cash weekly and set a clear point to change course if sales lag."),
     ("What makes a successful entrepreneur?",
      "Persistence, learning fast and managing money well. Most successful founders test ideas before betting everything, like Airbnb's founders renting air mattresses first, then adjust quickly based on what customers do. They know their numbers, especially cash flow and break-even, and they build trust through ethical, reliable service. Free mentoring from SCORE or a Small Business Development Center helps first-timers avoid common mistakes.")],
    [SBA, ["SCORE mentors", "https://www.score.org"], ["Census Business Formation Statistics", "https://www.census.gov/econ/bfs/index.html"]])

sub("biz-risk-profit", "What drives business profit and business risk", ["EC:010", "EC:011"],
    ["Profit is what's left after a business pays all its expenses: revenue minus costs. It rewards owners for taking risk and signals that a business is creating value customers will pay for. Profit depends on price (what customers will pay), sales volume (demand), costs (fixed and variable), efficiency and productivity, competition, and the wider environment: the economy, interest rates, taxes and regulation.",
     "Business risk is the chance that results fall short of expectations, or that the business fails. Sources include economic risk (recessions, inflation, interest rates), market and competitive risk (new rivals, changing tastes), operational risk (supply chain breakdowns, equipment failure), technological risk (disruption, cyberattacks), legal and regulatory risk, financial risk (too much debt), natural risk (disasters) and human risk (key-person loss, fraud, errors). Higher potential profit usually comes with higher risk."],
    ["Profit levers: raise price, sell more, cut variable costs, cut fixed costs, improve the product mix.",
     "Profit margin = profit / revenue; a 1-point margin gain on $1 million of sales is $10,000.",
     "Operating leverage: businesses with high fixed costs see profit swing more with sales.",
     "Financial leverage: debt boosts returns in good times and losses in bad times.",
     "Reduce risk by diversifying customers and suppliers, keeping cash reserves, insuring, using contracts and monitoring indicators."],
    ["The average U.S. grocery store runs on a net profit margin of only about 1% to 3%, so small cost changes matter a lot (industry estimates).",
     "Interest-rate changes affect profit directly through borrowing costs: with the Fed at 3.75%-4.00% and prime at 7.00% (Oct. 2026), variable-rate business loans cost more than in 2021."],
    ["Blockbuster's dominance disappeared after it passed on buying Netflix in 2000 and was slow to move to streaming, a case of technological and competitive risk.",
     "COVID-era supply chain disruptions in 2021-22 pushed many companies to keep more inventory and add second suppliers, trading some profit for less risk."],
    ["Profit isn't the same as revenue.", "Higher risk doesn't guarantee higher profit; it only makes higher profit possible."],
    "\"A 5% price increase on your best seller adds about $9,000 of profit a year without selling one more unit, the fastest profit lever you have.\"",
    [("How could our client increase profit without increasing sales?",
      "Work on price and costs. A small price increase on items where customers are loyal goes straight to profit: 5% on $180,000 of sales is $9,000. Then cut variable costs, by renegotiating supplier prices, reducing waste and adjusting portions or packaging. Finally, shift effort toward the highest-margin products. We'd test the price change on one product first and track the margin monthly."),
     ("What external factors could hurt this business, and how would you prepare?",
      "The economy, interest rates, competition and regulation. A recession would cut customer spending, so we'd keep three to six months of cash reserves. Higher rates raise borrowing costs, so we'd prefer fixed-rate financing. A new competitor could undercut prices, so we'd build loyalty through service. And we'd watch indicators like consumer spending and the jobs report monthly. Planning for the risks we can name makes the business resilient to the ones we can't.")],
    [SBA, ["BLS business survival data", "https://www.bls.gov/bdm/"]])
